// Effective block phase (0 → 1) for a given program week, remapped through a cycle block's
// phaseWindow. A cycle block can occupy a SLICE of the macro accumulate→intensify→realize arc (e.g.
// a 2-week "Realize" block tagged [0.8, 1]); honoring the window means that block is treated as that
// slice from its very first week — heavy from week 1, not re-accumulating from phase 0. This is the
// same remap computeCell already applies to the rep range; centralizing it here keeps RIR, the
// progression-style selector and the phase label in lockstep with the periodized rep range instead
// of contradicting it. No-op for standalone programs (no phaseWindow), so their phase is unchanged.
// singleWeekVal is the phase used for a 1-week block (callers historically used 0.5 or 1).

/* A single day can override the program's overall goal — e.g. a strength-focus Upper day inside an
   otherwise hypertrophy week, the way hybrid programs run Upper/Lower (strength) alongside a
   Push/Pull/Legs (hypertrophy) rotation. `day.focus` is "strength" | "hypertrophy"; unset follows
   the program goal. This is the one place the per-day goal is resolved, so every prescription
   (rep range, RIR, set count, rest) stays consistent. */

/* ============================== UTILITIES ============================== */


/* core day builder: distribute `total` exercise slots across weighted parts, then pick */
// High-skill lifts that no coach hands a brand-new trainee: technical hinges, exotic squat
// variants, explosive/olympic-adjacent moves and very hard regressions. Soft-penalized (not
// banned) for experience none/beginner so sparse home setups can still fall back to them.

/* Like-for-like swap ranking: same movement pattern first (swapping a pulldown surfaces other
   vertical pulls), then same type (replacing an isolation suggests isolations, not squats),
   then staple priority. Used by both the editor and the in-session swap sheet. */
/* Ranks alternatives for a swap. `starved` is the set of sub-regions currently BELOW their own MEV in
 * this program, and it outranks everything else — because it is the one thing the lifter cannot see
 * from the list and the most likely reason they opened it.
 *
 * The concrete failure: swapping an Arnold Press, the old order put every shoulder PRESS first
 * (same movement pattern), then every compound, and only then isolations — so a lateral raise landed
 * far down a long list and carried no "Similar" badge, because that badge fired only on an identical
 * movement pattern. But on a program whose front delts are already at their ceiling from benching and
 * pressing while side delts sit under their floor, a lateral is not a distant relative of an Arnold
 * press — it is the single most useful thing that slot could become. Pattern similarity answered
 * "what looks like what I'm replacing" when the question being asked is "what should go here".
 *
 * Order: fills a starved region → same movement pattern → same type → priority. Pattern still ranks
 * high because when nothing is starved, a like-for-like swap IS what the lifter wants. */


/* Sub-regions this program currently trains below their own MEV. Computed once per swap sheet. */
/* ⚠ A FAILURE HERE USED TO READ AS "NOTHING IS STARVED". This wrapped the whole body in
   `try { ... } catch {}` and returned an EMPTY SET on any throw — the most optimistic answer
   available, handed to callers deciding whether a muscle needs rescuing. Every silent failure in
   this project has cost a session precisely because it looked like good news: vacuous measurements
   that read as "no delta", inert engine rules that read as "no regression", a gate that could not
   fail and read 17/17. Let it throw; the gates will say so, and a loud failure is cheaper than a
   quiet wrong answer. */

// Fatigue COST used for the weekly axial budget: what a slot withdraws from the shared account.
/* ⚠ TAKES THE ENGINE, because the supported-row exemption lives in `isAxialLoad` and this is the
   SHARED OWNER every caller reads — gates/axialspacing included, deliberately so there is one
   definition of spinal cost. Without threading it, the gate measured the old rule while generation
   used the new one, and reported a breach the engine had already fixed. Defaults to the pre-28
   behaviour so no existing caller changes. */

/* ---- PRESS ANGLE -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * Day templates say WHICH MUSCLES a session emphasises and nothing about the ANGLE of the pressing.
 * The selector had no notion of it either — every bench variant classifies as pattern "press" — so a
 * day labelled "Push · Chest" could be anchored on an Incline Dumbbell Press while the flat Barbell
 * Bench Press landed on "Push · Delts", and a day literally called "Incline + Pull" could be given a
 * flat bench. The muscle accounting was correct in every one of those cases; the session just did not
 * match its own name, which is the kind of wrongness a lifter notices immediately and no volume gate
 * can see.
 *
 * Read from the NAME, which is where the app already encodes this (and where it already reads posture
 * from for machine setup fields). Names are stable, human-authored and reviewed — a derived signal
 * beats a new per-exercise field nobody will maintain across 349 entries. */
/* ── WHAT OWNING ONE PIECE OF KIT IMPLIES ABOUT ANOTHER ──────────────────────────────────────
   An adjustable bench sets flat. A lifter who ticks "Adjustable / Incline Bench" and not "Flat
   Bench" has not told you they cannot bench flat — they have told you they own the bench that does
   both. Without this, that selection silently removed every flat press from their program.

   The two entries were also both labelled "Adjustable ..." until now, so the pair read as
   duplicates and inviting exactly one tick. Reported by a lifter whose incline day kept opening on
   a flat barbell bench: `inclinebench` was simply absent from their gym, so no incline press
   existed to choose and the day fell back correctly to the wrong-looking answer.

   Deliberately one-directional: a flat bench does NOT imply an adjustable one. */

/* The next-engine adapter maps engine picks into this catalog; it must judge "can this gym do it?" with THESE rules.
   Registered once, at load (function declarations are hoisted, so this is safe here). See app-shell-adapter.ts. */


/* ---- ANCHOR LOADABILITY ----------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * The PRESCRIPTION has always been goal-aware per day (goalForDay → rep range, RIR, rest, set
 * count). SELECTION was not aware of the goal at all, so a strength or peak block was handed
 * strength sets and reps on whatever lift the hypertrophy scorer liked best. Measured: a
 * goal:"strength" program at a fully equipped gym anchored its four days on Dumbbell Bench Press,
 * Lat Pulldown, Hack Squat and Dumbbell Shoulder Press — not one barbell main lift, and the exact
 * same four picks the hypertrophy program got. 31.0% barbell anchors for hypertrophy, both AND
 * strength alike: the goal moved the number by nothing at all.
 *
 * The cause is a collision between two anchor bonuses. Engine 3 gives a lengthened-position
 * candidate -1.25 at the anchor — deliberately, and it is what lets an RDL or a hack squat open a
 * day for a muscle that would otherwise see no long-length loading all week — while the
 * barbell-main-lift bonus is only -1.0. `db-bench` and `hack-squat` are both in the lengthened
 * table, so they beat `bb-bench` and `back-squat` by 0.25 on every seed and every split.
 *
 * For a hypertrophy block that ordering is correct and is left alone. For a strength or peak block
 * it answers the wrong question: the main lift there is chosen for LOADABILITY — the barbell you can
 * add 2.5 kg to every week and test at the end — not for stimulus per set. You cannot peak a hack
 * squat, and the strength standards, training maxes and e1RM carryover are all keyed to the barbell
 * lifts. anchorLoad scales the barbell-anchor bonus by the day's own goal so each block answers its
 * own question. Accessory slots are untouched, so long-length coverage is simply re-earned there:
 * the -1.25 still stands for every slot after the anchor. */
/* How much of the 48h axial-carry charge a load-emphasis day's MAIN LIFT is forgiven (scaled by
   anchorLoad, so a hypertrophy day is forgiven none of it). Swept 0.6 / 0.8 / 1.0 over 5,598 days:
   the axial numbers are IDENTICAL at all three (zero days over 3.0 of spinal spend, zero days with
   two bilateral barbell squats) and barbell anchors move by ~3pp at goal "both" and not at all at
   goal "strength". Nothing is bought by forgiving more, so take the smallest value that works — the
   recovery model keeps 40% of its say over the main lift instead of none. */
/* How far the set rows are pulled out past the exercise card's own padding so the active-set band
   reaches the screen edges. Must match that padding, or the band stops short on one side. */
/* Are these the same program as far as the LIFTER is concerned? Deliberately ignores `updatedAt`:
   that is sync bookkeeping, stamped onto every record by stampedSetter so cross-device merges can tell
   which copy is newer. It is not content, and comparing it made a committed program differ from itself
   forever — the saved copy came back stamped, the open copy was not, so "unsaved changes" never
   cleared and Back would have prompted on every exit. Any future sync-only field belongs in this list
   too.

   ⚠ AND SO DOES ANYTHING THE APP WRITES TO THE PROGRAM ON THE LIFTER'S BEHALF. HAIDEN: "It keeps
   telling me I updated my program after starting a workout and exiting." He had edited nothing.
   `startUpNext` calls `autoregulate`, which returns a NEW program object carrying `autoBias` (the
   volume tweak recomputed from recent performance and recovery feedback) and `autoTuned` (the up/down
   summary the session briefly shows), then `setProgram` writes it into the OPEN copy while `saved[]`
   is left alone. This deep-compare then found two fields differing and reported unsaved changes.
   MEASURED, and it is these two and nothing else: driving a real session and leaving it, the diff
   between the stored program and the draft is exactly ['autoBias','autoTuned'].

   THEY ARE NOT EDITS AND CANNOT BE SAVED MEANINGFULLY. autoBias is ephemeral by design — the comment
   at its call site says so, it is cleared and rebuilt from scratch before every session precisely so
   it can never compound, so "Update" would only bake in a number the next session immediately
   recomputes. autoTuned is a one-session display note. Neither survives a session, so neither can be
   the thing an "unsaved changes" prompt is asking the lifter to decide about.

   THE GENERAL SHAPE, because this is the second time: a blanket stringify means ANY field the app
   later writes to the program at session time reintroduces the prompt. The list below is the seam —
   if you add a field the lifter did not author, it goes here. gates/draftfalse.mjs asserts the
   behaviour (start a session, leave, nothing is dirty) rather than these two names, so a third such
   field fails the gate instead of shipping. */

// Preview and commit share the same output; the UI never estimates sibling impact.


 // matches the exercise pane's 16px horizontal padding
/* GENERATION ATTRIBUTION — off by default, and off is the normal state.
   `explainGeneration` switches this on, RE-RUNS the same generateProgram with the program's own
   stored seed, and switches it off again. Generation is synchronous and single-threaded, so a module
   flag is safe here and costs nothing when null: every recording site is guarded, and the scoring
   arithmetic is byte-identical either way because each helper returns the value it was handed and the
   operators and their order are untouched.
   RE-RUNNING rather than storing a trace on the program is deliberate. A stored trace would be a
   second copy of the truth that ages the moment the engine changes, and would bloat every saved
   program in the store. A re-run is generated by the CURRENT engine and can be checked against the
   program it claims to explain — which gates/genexplain.mjs does, because an attribution of a
   different program than the one on screen is worse than none. */
/* ═══ PIPELINE TRACE — WHAT EACH PASS DID, NOT JUST WHAT WAS PICKED ════════════════════════════
 * `GEN_TRACE` below answers "why this exercise" — the scoring terms behind one selection.
 * This answers a different question that nothing could: "which PASS changed my program, and to
 * what?" Generation is a chain of nine mutating passes over one object, and until now the only way
 * to see between them was to hand-patch console.logs into the source, rebuild, and delete them
 * afterwards. That was done twice in a single session, and both times it found the bug within one
 * run — a pulling movement placed by the coverage floor and cut straight back out by the time
 * fitter, and later the fact that engine 8's losses were in SELECTION rather than trimming, which
 * three reasoned attempts had failed to work out. Temporary instrumentation that keeps being
 * rebuilt is a permanent feature that has not been written yet.
 *
 * Null unless a trace is running, so the cost to normal generation is one property read per stage. */
/* ═══ REDUCE — ONE NUMBER, BECAUSE THERE WERE TWO AND THEY DISAGREED BY HALF ═══════════════════
 * A de-emphasised muscle was damped to 0.3 of its weight in the day plan and separately floored at
 * 0.6 of MEV by the coverage pass. Two owners for one lever, and the weight runs first, so 0.3 won
 * and the floor spent the rest of generation trying to claw back a muscle the plan had already
 * starved. Measured before this change: reducing a SINGLE muscle took it to ZERO in 237 of 840
 * programs — 28% — and `auditProgramWeek` never noticed, because a group with any sibling volume
 * left does not read as absent.
 *
 * ⚠ AND THE LEVER WAS THE WRONG TYPE, WHICH IS THE DEEPER POINT. A selection WEIGHT has no defined
 * relationship to an outcome: `w *= 0.3` means "this muscle loses apportionment contests", which
 * lands anywhere between a mild reduction and nothing at all depending on what else happens to be
 * competing that day. An input-side lever cannot make an output-side promise, and "train my legs
 * less" is entirely an output-side promise. So the weight is now derived FROM the volume target
 * rather than chosen independently of it: one fraction, stated once, meaning the same thing in both
 * places.
 *
 * NOT a reallocation mechanism. Freed capacity returns to the general pool and is spent only if some
 * other claim actually wants it — a muscle under MEV, or a focus point the lifter spent. If nothing
 * wants it the session is simply shorter, which is the honest outcome for someone who asked for less
 * work. Unconditional reallocation measured worse: it left minutes per day unchanged and took the
 * week from 70 to 75 total sets, because leg slots became cheaper upper-body slots. Someone who
 * asked for less got more, and no ceiling was breached, so nothing would ever have flagged it. */

/* ⚠ MRV_GRAIN — ONE OWNER FOR "IS THIS WEEK OVER MRV", BECAUSE THERE WERE TWO AND THEY DISAGREED.
 *
 * `capWeeklyVolume` stops trimming once the worst muscle is within half a set of its ceiling.
 * `volumeZone` called anything above the ceiling "over MRV" with no tolerance at all. So the
 * generator would deliberately leave a week at 20.4 sets against an MRV of 20, and the app would
 * then tell the lifter that same week was over MRV — and `volumeAdvice` would answer it with a
 * two-set cut on someone who had done exactly what the plan said. That is the v616 report's root
 * cause, and it is the v584 divergence defect: a shared rule that most callers adopted and one kept
 * a private copy of.
 *
 * THE TOLERANCE IS NOT A FUDGE, IT IS THE GRANULARITY OF THE ONLY LEVER THERE IS. Volume moves one
 * whole set at a time, while weekly volume is fractional because secondary credit is fractional. An
 * overshoot smaller than half a set therefore has no move that improves it: trimming hamstrings from
 * 20.4 to 19.4 to satisfy a ceiling of 20 lands 0.6 BELOW, a bigger error than the one being fixed,
 * and costs the lifter a real working set to do it. A deadband narrower than the lever is incoherent.
 *
 * MEASURED before choosing this over tightening the cap: across 756 generated programs, 29 have a
 * part above its ceiling and ALL 29 are under half a set over. Zero at 0.5-1, zero at 1-2, zero
 * beyond. There is no case in the whole sweep of the ceiling pass genuinely failing to bite — the
 * ceiling was already correct and the reporting was wrong, which is the opposite of what "fix plan
 * generation" sounds like and the reason to measure the distribution before writing the fix. */

 /* unused while the weight path is held — see the note at the apportionment */
/* WEEKLY SCHEDULER — hard sets to (or near) failure on the same muscle two calendar days running
   blunts recovery and performance. Muscle protein synthesis from a hard session runs ~36–48h, so
   the same muscle shouldn't be hammered on back-to-back days. This lays the program's N training
   days across a 7-day week, ordering them so adjacent days share as little direct-muscle load as
   possible, and filling the leftover days with rest. The result is a recommended `weekPlan`:
   7 slots, each either a day index or null (rest). Pure logic, fully testable, no UI required. */


 // see buildWeekPlan — priced on dayOverlap's real scale (median 10.3)

/* ⚠ `loadOf` IS A PARAMETER SO THERE IS ONE SEARCH, NOT TWO. Engine 11 needs this same
   overlap-minimising order BEFORE exercises are selected, and the obvious way to get it — a second
   copy scoring template part-weights instead of chosen lifts — is the v584 divergence defect by
   construction: two orderings that agree today and drift the first time either is tuned. The
   search, the tie-break and IDEAL_SLOTS stay here; only the load function varies. */
/* ⚠ ENGINE 11: THE SEARCH SCORES THE PAIRS THAT ARE ACTUALLY 24 HOURS APART, NOT A UNIFORM RING.
   The original objective summed overlap over every consecutive pair around the cycle, weighted
   equally, and fixed day 0 in place on the stated grounds that "every rotation of this same order
   has the identical total overlap — there's no remaining slot-position-specific optimization to do."
   That is true of a ring. It is FALSE of a calendar, because the slots are not evenly spaced:
   IDEAL_SLOTS[5] is [0,1,3,4,6], whose gaps are 1,2,1,2 and 1 at the wrap. Only THREE of the five
   pairs are genuine back-to-back days, and which day-pair lands in one of them depends entirely on
   the rotation the search had already decided not to consider.
   Measured: torso_limbs/5/s60 ships a worst adjacent overlap of 0.82 where a brute force over all
   120 orders finds 0.51; the same split at goal `both` ships 0.73 against 0.22. The mechanism was
   optimising a quantity nobody experiences while the one a lifter feels — training the same muscles
   on two consecutive days — went unminimised.
   So engine 11 scores overlap ONLY across true 24h gaps, and permutes all n days rather than fixing
   the first, because rotation is now load-bearing. n ≤ 6 here, so 720 arrangements at worst.
   Engine 10 and below keep the ring objective exactly, including the fixed first day. */
/* ⚠ `eng` USED TO DEFAULT TO 0, AND A CALLER THAT FORGOT IT GOT A DIFFERENT WEEK. Engine 11's
   tight-pair objective and engine 13's repeat penalty are both gated on it, so `buildWeekPlan(p)`
   silently returned the pre-11 ordering: measured on full_body_patterns/5/s60, the stored plan is
   [3,2,null,0,1,null,4] and a default-engine recompute gives [0,1,null,2,4,null,3] — a completely
   different week from the same program. Reported by Haiden as "the calendar version for suggested is
   completely different than what it suggested". The default is now the PROGRAM'S OWN engine, which is
   the only correct answer; generation still passes `eng` explicitly and is unaffected. */

